  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWe are watching for some patchy dense fog again this morning. So \nfar, based on satellite and area cameras and visibility sensors, \nwhat is out there now appears to be lower level cloud cover in the \nEastern Highlands. We are also watching some increasing cloud cover \nmove in from the west, so we will see if/how this cloud cover \nimpacts fog development this morning. Overall confidence is how much \nfog we see and where exactly it sets up is low. The cloud cover \nmentioned is moving in ahead of approaching troughing in the Pacific \nNorthwest. This troughing moves through Idaho this evening and \novernight and brings a cold front with it. This cold front ushers in \nshowers, mountain snow, gusty winds, and cooler temperatures. \nShowers arrive in the Central Mountains this afternoon and spread \neastward overnight into Tuesday, bringing total QPF in the lower \nelevations to around a tenth of an inch or less and as you go up in \nelevation, look for totals to be closer to 0.20 to 0.30 inches. Most \nof the accumulating snow will be above 6500 feet this time around \nwith about 1-3\" in the forecast. Between 6000 and 6500 feet some \nsnow will likely mix in, but accumulations will be light, if at all. \nAs temperatures fall to near or just below freezing in the Snake \nRiver Plain early Tuesday, we could even see a few flurries mix in, \nbut accumulation is not expected. Wind will also be an impact with \nthis cold front. Wind gusts increase to 20 to 30 mph by this \nafternoon with stronger gusts of 30-40 mph are expected with FROPA \nand just behind the front, especially overnight into Tuesday. These \nwinds are just below Wind Advisory criteria with about a 15 to 20 \npercent chance of gusts reaching 45 mph out across the INL area and \n40 to 50 percent chance of reaching 45 mph in the higher elevations \nin the South Hills and just east of Idaho Falls. The potential for \ntravel impacts due to 45 mph wind gusts is highest along I-84 from \nthe 84/86 split down to the Utah state line, our typical windy down-\nsloping spot. Of course, the cold front will drop temperatures about \n10 degrees from today to tomorrow. Meaning most will go from mid 50s \nto mid 60s today to mid 40s to mid 50s on Tuesday afternoon. \n\nFor the most part, Tuesday afternoon through Thursday morning will \nbe mainly dry and temperatures will slowly warm up again into the \nmid 50s and low 60s. A stronger low will begin to impact Eastern \nIdaho on Friday and bring showers on and off throughout the weekend \nalong with some cooler temperatures again. There are still details \nfor the models to iron out with how this system will move and how \nmuch precipitation we will actually see.",
